<sn><md>### Elemental Composition of Proteins

Proteins are composed of **five major elements**: `CHONS`

| Element | Percentage Range |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Carbon** | 50–55% |
| **Hydrogen** | 6–7.3% |
| **Oxygen** | 19–24% |
| **Nitrogen** | 13–19% |
| **Sulfur** | 0–4% |

In addition to these five core elements, some proteins may also contain trace elements such as **phosphorus, iron, copper, iodine, magnesium, manganese, or zinc**.

</md></sn><sn><md>### Amino Acid

* **Definition:** An amino acid is an organic compound containing both a basic **amino group $-NH_2$** and an acidic **carboxylic group $-COOH$**.

* **Basic Components:** Every standard amino acid contains 
   :::.row
   :::.col-12 .col-lg-8
   - an $\alpha$-carbon attached to an amino group, 
   - a carboxylic acid group, 
   - a hydrogen atom, and 
   - a variable side chain represented as the **$R$ group**.
   :::

   :::.col-12 .col-lg-4
   ![img](https://bio.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/8209/aminoacid.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=550&amp;height=543)
   :::
   :::

* **Standard Amino Acids:** Although over *300 amino acids* exist in nature, `20 standard amino acids` form proteins.

* **Zwitterion:** In ++aqueous environments++, amino acids exist in a stable dipolar form called a `zwitterion`. 

   In this state, the amino group accepts a proton $-NH_3^+$ while the carboxyl group donates a proton $-COO^-$, making the molecule `overall electrically neutral` despite possessing both positive and negative charges.
   ![img](https://sciencequery.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/zwitterion.png)
</md></sn><sn><md>### Amino Acid Classification

| Category | Count | Amino Acids | Notes |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Essential Amino Acids** | 10 | Lysine, Valine, Isoleucine, Threonine, Tryptophan, Histidine, Arginine, Leucine, Methionine, Phenylalanine | Cannot be synthesized by the body; must be obtained through diet. Mnemonic: **L VITTAL MP** |
| **Semi-Essential Amino Acids** | 2 | Arginine, Histidine | Required in higher amounts during specific developmental stages (e.g., infancy). |
| **Non-Essential Amino Acids** | 10 | Alanine, Glycine, Cysteine, Asparagine, Glutamine, Tyrosine, Proline, Glutamic acid, Aspartic acid, Serine | Can be synthesized by the body; dietary intake is not mandatory. |

*Note: Arginine and Histidine appear in both the Essential and Semi-Essential categories depending on the classification system used.*
</md></sn><sn><md>Four Hierarchical Levels of 
####  **Protein Structure**
Protein structures are complex and organized into `four hierarchical levels`:

#### Primary Structure $1^\circ$:

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-8
* Represents the `linear sequence of amino acids` connected by ++peptide bonds++ within a *polypeptide chain*.
* It possesses an `N-terminus` (amino end) and a `C-terminus` (carboxyl end).
* The `precise sequence` dictates the **identity and function** of the protein; altering even a single amino acid can change the entire protein's function.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-4
![img](https://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcS_9gvxB00rlUeVCrIYZnue-YR8wkP7Fq7DMz_2X2YoscC2jnWI7M8osBRb&amp;s=10)
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>### **Secondary Structure $2^\circ$:**

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-9
* Refers to local `folding or coiling` of the polypeptide backbone, stabilized by `hydrogen bonds` formed between ++nearby amino acids++.
* **Two major forms:**
  * $\alpha$-Helix:** A `right-handed clockwise` coil common in structural proteins like ++keratin++ (found in hair and nails).
  * $\beta$-Pleated Sheet:** Polypeptide chains `running side-by-side in parallel` or anti-parallel orientations, joined by `hydrogen bonds` (found in silk protein, **fibroin**).
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-3
![img](https://microbenotes.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/Secondary-Structure-of-Protein.jpeg)
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>### Tertiary Structure $3^\circ$

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-8
* The overall **three-dimensional (3D) shape** of a single polypeptide chain formed by `folding and bending`.
* Determined by `interactions between the R` side chains.
* Governs the `biological activity` of the protein and determines whether it is `fibrous or globular`.
* **Bonds involved:** 
 Hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, disulfide bridges, hydrophobic interactions, and Van der Waals forces.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-4
![img](https://studymind.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/Screen-Shot-2022-03-29-at-12.52.48-PM.png)
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>#### Quaternary Structure $4^\circ$

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-8
* The `spatial arrangement` and assembly of `two or more polypeptide chains (subunits)` forming a single functional protein complex.
* Held together by the `same non-covalent and covalent` interactions found in tertiary structures.
* **Key Examples:**
  * **Hemoglobin:** Composed of **4 subunits** $2\alpha$ chains and $2\beta$ chains).
  * **Insulin:** Composed of **2 subunits** (A and B chains) linked by disulfide bridges.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-4
![img](https://themedicalbiochemistrypage.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/04/hemoglobin.jpg)
:::
:::



</md></sn><sn><md>#### Proteins

**functions**

Proteins `occur naturally` in all living organisms as `complex chains of amino acids` joined by peptide bonds. They form the core structural material of cells, act as metabolic catalysts (enzymes), and are found abundantly in both plant and animal matter.

**Key Biological Functions**

*   **Structure:** `Collagen and keratin` build skin, bone, hair, and connective tissues.Build and repair tissue.
*   **Catalysis:** `Enzymes` accelerate vital metabolic and chemical reactions.Speed up chemical reactions.
*   **Regulation &amp; Defense:** Peptide `hormones` (like insulin) and` antibodies` protect and regulate bodily systems.Seng chemical signals.


---

**Natural Sources of Protein**

+++ ...
![protien_sources](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Soil%20Biochemistry/Protein/assets/protien_sources.jpeg)

+++

*   **Animal Sources:** Meat, poultry, fish, seafood, eggs, and `dairy products` provide complete proteins with all essential amino acids.
*   **Plant Sources:** Legumes (beans, lentils), nuts, seeds, and whole grains (quinoa) supply abundant base-based proteins.
*   **Abundant Biopolymers:** The `enzyme RuBisCO` is the most abundant protein in nature, driving carbon fixation in plants.



**What Determines Protein Lifespan?**

The amount of time a protein sticks around is dependent on several factors.

*   **Size:** `Larger proteins` tend to ++degrade faster++.
*   **Shape:** Proteins are `folded in such a way` that only `certain areas` of the protein are `accessible`. This can change over time as proteins interact with one another, changing how the protein functions within the cell. `Shape changes` can also reveal protein characteristics which make them more likely to get degraded. One example of this is `misfolding`.

*   **Stopping activity:** Proteins are `important messengers` for cells, transferring information to and from different cell areas. ++Cells++ are built to `turn these signals off` after they are turned on. One way of doing this is to `remove the protein` responsible for sending the signal.

